{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And YHWH said: The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah</b> This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 18:20): 'And YHWH said to Abraham' — meaning that He did as He had said, that He would not conceal it from him. And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 18:10) said that the verse 'And the men turned from there' was inserted in between, to inform that at the time they came to Sodom, then HaShem said to Abraham, 'The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah, for it is great.' And so is the opinion of all the commentators, that He was speaking with Abraham. According to this, the correct understanding of the verse 'And the men turned from there' is to say that when HaShem said to Abraham as the men departed from him, 'The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah, for it is great,' Abraham stood in prayer and supplication before Him to forgive them and to grant him permission to speak, and he prolonged his prayer until the men came to Sodom, and then Abraham approached and said, 'Will You indeed sweep away?' Or its meaning may be that he returned to the wellspring — 'he was still standing before YHWH' — for Abraham approached and said, 'Will You indeed sweep away.' And behold, he prolonged his supplication before Him each time, saying, 'Let not the anger of YHWH be kindled,' and each time he was directing his mind toward prophecy until he heard a response to his words from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, and they prolonged in this the entire day, and YHWH went in the evening when He finished speaking with Abraham, and the two angels came to Sodom." "<b>The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah</b> — it is the outcry of the oppressed, who cry out and scream from the tyranny of their wickedness. And it would have been fitting for the verse to say, 'The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah I have heard, for it is great,' or to say, 'The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah is great and their sin is very heavy.' But the meaning of the verse is: 'The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah and their sin, which has grown very great — I will go down and see whether they have all sinned therein, and if not, I will know who the sinners are.' And regarding the matter of the descent and the seeing, Rashi said by way of derash: it teaches that cases involving capital punishment are not concluded except by seeing. And by way of peshat: because HaShem wished to reveal to Abraham the matter of Sodom and to inform him that there is none among them who does good, He said to him, 'The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah, for it is great; I will go down to see' — that is to say, I have come to judge: if they have sinned, I will bring destruction upon them, and if not, I will know what I will do to them, and I will visit the rod of their transgression and with plagues for their iniquity. He informed him that their sentence was not yet final, and that now He would visit their iniquity upon them and judge them. And this is like the language of 'YHWH from heaven looked down upon the children of men, to see if there were any who understood, who sought Elohim — all have turned aside, together they have become corrupt' (Psalms 14:2–3). And Rabbi Abraham said in it a secret — those who deliver to strangers will suffice for it. And I will hint to you the view of the receivers of truth: our Sages (Jerusalemite Talmud, Ta'anit ch. 2) expounded the verse 'For behold, YHWH is going forth from His place, and will come down, and will tread upon the high places of the earth' (Micah 1:3) — He goes forth and comes from measure to measure, departing from the measure of mercy and entering the measure of judgment. And so is this matter: 'And YHWH said to His heart: The outcry of Sodom and Gomorrah, for it is great; I will go down' — from the measure of mercy to the measure of judgment (Zohar 105a), 'and I will see' — with mercy, whether, as her outcry that comes before Me by way of the measure of judgment, they have done destruction (ibid. 106a), 'and if not, I will know' and I will have compassion, in the manner of 'And Elohim knew' (Exodus 2:25). And after the verse related the knowledge of the Most High, it returned to the first matter and related in the narrative that the men who had looked upon the face of Sodom to go there, and whom Abraham had sent off — they arrived there; and Abraham, from the time they parted from him until they arrived there, was still standing before Him, for He had called him and informed him that the angels — they are the ones sent for this, to destroy the place, as He had said. And there was no need to explain when he stood before Him, for from the time He said, 'Shall I conceal from Abraham?' it was known that He had told him."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The phrase 'returned to the wellspring' (ששב לבאר) is unusual; translated hyper-literally as it appears. It may refer to returning to the prophetic source or to the verse's reference point.", 1 "The phrase 'those who deliver to strangers will suffice for it' (מילדי נכרים יספיקו בו) is obscure; translated hyper-literally. It appears to be Ibn Ezra's cryptic reference to certain allegorists or philosophical interpreters; the precise referent is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:31:42.793790874Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}